  {
    "episode_id": 49431,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49431.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "So I started going to San Miguel, Mora, and Colfax. Really the first week, the second week that the fires started, partially because I was in Colfax announcing with the Undersecretary a wonderful program that's pilot for New Mexico. That's the World Partners Network that USDA is going to fund three individuals. to work with the counties of Colfax, Mora, Taos and San Miguel to be able to access federal resources. This has been a big issue of mine of needing to get help to our world communities so they can access the federal programs, the funds that are available because we know that that's hard to do. And that's exactly the issue here is that people are having a hard time accessing existing federal funds. So I'm up there at an announcement regarding that we have the four counties represented and the smoke is billowing and it is growing. So I started going to the collaborators meetings with the incident commanders, my staff has participated the entire time. We are in our fourth month, and I can tell you in that second week and that third week and that fourth week, fifth week, the look on people's faces, the fear never left them, but the concern, and they were showing up at those meetings. And I would think, are their eyes red because they've not slept? are they read because the smoke is burning everybody's eyes, but they were there. So from the beginning, I saw how scary this was, but also the response of the community, the response of the sheriff's office, you know, of the local officials of those who could help, showing up at those collaborative meetings to see what they could do. You know, I was meeting with the commissioners of Moda County when basically they had not yet been evacuated from the building, but so many of the other communities that they love had been evacuated and we were there meeting discussing issues where all they were fielding, oh no, this community needs to leave, that community needs to leave. And then I've been back there to look at the burn scars. I've been back there to visit what preparations they've been doing on the flooding, to prevent the flooding, and where we've seen the flooding. And then lastly, I went back there for the alternative Moira fiesas, because they weren't able to have their fiesas, because they were evacuated. And it was nice to be with the Hinte dancing and the band's playing. And",
