    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49431.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "Everything, right? But we need to bring the federal resources to build back. We have to rise from the ashes, as we keep saying, right? We need to rise from the ashes. And the only way you do that given The calamity and the extent of the damage is by bringing all resources that we can find federal state. There's not a lot of local because they've been wiped out but federal state and I will say philanthropic and community resources to help out. Right now, you know, one of the big issues has been looking at how do we provide a place and a way for ranchers to get the cattle fed, because as we've heard the fire destroyed mountain pasture land, the ranchers have the cattle where they would normally be growing the feed because they could pasture the cattle there. So the multiplier effect, the domino effect is significant. And so that's something we need to do. I have immediate concern is to continue to work to mitigate the flooding to both clean up after the flooding and to do what we can to prevent it. And then of course, ground water and surface water that is used for drinking is contaminated or at risk of contamination. And so those to me are the most urgent is the water and then, you know, making sure that people don't lose any more of their resources like the cattle or their crop because much of the bottom land dip burn or the dip burn severely and so it could still be tilled and farmed, but not if you actually have to have your cattle there, right?",
