  {
    "episode_id": 49431,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49431.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "So one, because, you know, and I say it over and over again, and everybody in the community says it over again, because we know who started this fire. And so we know whose responsibility it is to help to make this whole right. The Forest Service Fire, which is the federal government, and so they have a different kind of responsibility in this crisis than normal. FEMA, when it is dealing with natural disasters, is not intended to make you whole. What it's intended to do is to come in and help lessen the pain, help you get back on your feet after a flood or hurricane or tornado. You know, it's like a helping hand, a federal government can come and say, I'm really sorry, let's help clean up the debris. Let's make sure you get housing assistance that you have somewhere to live. But they're not going to say, oh, I'm so sorry, you lost your $200,000 home. Here is $200,000. They'll say you lost your $200,000 home. Here's $37,000 to help you get started again. But this was not an actual disaster. This was a manmade disaster because they started both fires. So from the beginning, it has been my position. It has been the delegation position, you know, the two senators, myself, Melanie, The governor have always said, federal government, you need to pay for 100%. of the cost of this disaster. But what that addresses is the regular FEMA help. And so we got the federal government to say for fighting the fires, 100% federal. We're not going to ask the state or the local to pay anything, 100%, because normally the local government has to pay 25%. No, in this instance, federal government's paying 100%. Then we come in on the FEMA programs. And those programs are limited by statute, but they help. So people can get assistance for rental assistance or for home repair. They can get assistance if they got damage from flooding or more flows or debris. They can get assistance if we lost anybody. We were very fortunate that during the fire we did, but we know that we lost nine beautiful souls. And that's heartbreaking right after the fires. Moving in storage costs, unemployment assistance, crisis counseling, that's available now. And so FEMA, if you look at their reports, they have actually helped a lot of people in that regard. And they've gotten significant money out to the community to assist with that. So that's really great. then there's another important thing that needs to be done. One was battling the fire and now it's battling the floods. On public lands, the federal government is taking up 100% of the responsibility to be building the firms, to be building the diversions, to be working as they can to mitigate the flooding. But we have to remember, this is just under half a million acres. So that's a lot of land to go and work on to prevent the flooding. But that's public land. There is beautiful Moira has more private land in New Mexico than I think any other county. That's a beautiful thing that so many ranchers in Moira own the land and it's not owned by the Forest Service. So we needed to make sure that they get help with the flooding. We were able to get 100% that the federal government will pay for the cost of flood prevention and mitigation through the USDA. But this is one of those areas where I think we are running into the reimbursement issue. You know, people started raising that with management. Oh my goodness. So I am in discussions with community foundations, with philanthropists, with the state government of what can you do to basically issue in a sense of revolving loan fund, right? Because this is money that the feds are going to pay back, but we need to get money in people's pockets today to be able to advance those costs. So that is something that I am working on that takes a little longer because it's not just, okay, let's get a commitment from the federal government to go from 75% to 100. This is actually finding a mechanism and funding a mechanism to do that. And I think we will get something from the state of New Mexico. I've had very promising conversations with the governor and with legislators. Two things we have to see if there's something existing that they could use that they've already been authorized. If not, we have to wait for the legislature to come into session in January, which is too late. But we know that that's the outer end. But I am continuing to brainstorm. And if anybody is listening, join me in figuring out how we deal with this reimbursement issue because that's in statute. So that's harder.",
