    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, you know, this is such an example of this idea that they always say the devil is in the details. It's like, it sounds great. There's all this money. But then, I mean, and one of the words that keeps coming up, every single person I talk to about this issue, use the word capacity. And the problem they lay out is that there isn't the capacity, meaning human beings, on the part of the government or nonprofits to get the money from the federal government to the people on the ground and that's what you were talking about when we started out and it's a great idea in theory that you know it's like oh we want the money to go straight from the government to the people on the ground who need it without a middleman But the people who need it aren't necessarily able to fill out and manage complicated grants and navigate bureaucracies, not to mention the fact that a lot of people are pretty demoralized. There's a sense of mistrust of the federal government. And, you know, not to mention some people don't even know there's the money available. And so this question of capacity, this question of staffing, again, the folks I've been talking to would love to see a kind of long term capacity on the ground so that it's not just responding to crises when they happen, but really building back this land and having a long term vision for the watersheds, for the soil, for the resilience that would prevent these kinds of disasters in the longer run.",
