  {
    "episode_id": 49431,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49431.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "So I completely agree with that. That's why I'm very excited about the World Partners Network because that is intended to bring full-time staffing to be able to work with those four counties to be able to address needs and to help bring that money down. I'm also in conversations with the state of New Mexico that they need to set up an office that's fully funded. that has people who will be providing technical assistance to help both write the grants and oversee the grants. Because people don't have that, right? I raise it all the time because what we did in Congress was pass the Infrastructure and Jobs Act. $1.7 trillion, folks, and a lot of that could come to New Mexico, but only if we write the grants to bring it down. So this is something where if we get the state to invest in four people, five people, you know, whatever that would cost, they're going to get a return that is 100-fold and that will start making these differences on the ground. In the Infrastructure and Jobs Act, we also put significant money in for resiliency for partnerships between the Forest Service and tries between Forest Service and their neighbors, which in Moira are private landowners. And so we have resources available that I want to translate into reality on the ground in New Mexico. Now, as a congresswoman, we have to remember what my role is. And my role is getting that bill passed. My role is being the voice of the hente to like say, I know this is needed. So even though this isn't something the feds. can do. It's something the state can do. So I am being the voice of the hand there when I go and talk to the governor about it. I'm being the voice of the hand there when I go and talk to the legislators about it. Or when I talk to community foundations, because I can't set it up. I am just a conduit of everybody's concern of the sour of their pain. I understand that's my job is to take that sour to take that pain and turn it into action. It's not enough to just listen to it. I need to turn it into action. And that's why I'm working on, you know, we won again into the problem of these are long term solutions and we have a lot of short term hurt. So we really need to make sure that people are aware of the short term help that's available through USDA, through the state programs. and through FEMA. A medium-term problem is my Hermos Peak CAF Canyon Fire Assistance Act.",
