  {
    "episode_id": 49431,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49431.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, so I introduced the Hermos Peak Fire Assistance Act, basically to say, this wasn't a natural disaster, regular FEMA shouldn't apply. This was a man-made disaster, federal government start, federal government needs to pay for it. And it would do those things that we are talking about needing now. It would compensate you if you lost your ranch, your barns. It would compensate you if you lost your generational home. Because we know that a lot of people have their generational home, the home that they go and they spend time there in the summers on weekends, but they might have their other primary residence. in Las Vegas or Rio Rancho or somewhere else. And FEMA doesn't help you if it was not your quote unquote primary residence. This is more important than your primary residence. This is a generational home. You will get paid for that. You will get paid for business losses. You will get paid for the fact that You might harvest Christmas trees from your land and sell them and that's a major component of your income. You will get paid because we burnt down those Christmas trees. So you will get paid for all of those losses. You will get paid to help reforest and we vegetate your land. You'll get paid because you lost subsistence hunting because let's remember, when I was growing up, we always had a cow from the ranch in our freezer and a deer or an elk. That's what got us through the winter, right? It's delicious. Well, that's what people in Moana and San Miguel also do, is they not only hunt for their own food, but they sell those permits. That's a business loss. When they lost the ability to sell those permits because the elk and the deer are not gonna be grazing on burnt land, that's an income loss that I want them to be paid for. So as you can see, it covers what people need. We might not have gotten everything, but we tried. And I got it out of the house, twice. So I put it in the National Defense Authorization Act, because that's one of these bills that usually actually passes both the House and the Senate, because it's for defense. We always fund defense. I want us to fund defense and the people of San Miguel and Moda County. That is over in the Senate right now. I need to tell everybody. I am so proud of my colleagues. I went to them with this story. I shared with them what had happened. I shared with them the report that showed that the Forest Service made every mistake possible. They should have never lit that match. They failed over and over again. And that report proves it. And so I shared that with them. And they were like, we are here for you, Teresa. We are here for the people of San Miguel and Moira, bipartisan, overwhelming support for me to put my bill as an amendment into the NDAA. I am grateful to my colleagues because they backed me up. We now need to get it over the line in the Senate because the president has already said he'd sign it. So now the battle for that has moved to the Senate. I know Senator Lujan is doing everything he can. I had a meeting with both Senator Lujan and Senator Heinrich. We discussed this bill. We discussed strategy. They're working hard to make it happen. You know, so I am hopeful that we will get it. And that will make a huge difference because that's not reimbursement that's by all your claim. And there is a set amount of time by which FEMA we're doing it through FEMA because they're going to set up a dedicated office to this if when we pass it. There is a specific amount of time that they have to actually respond and pay the claim. So that's the other thing is that we won't be waiting for three years or four years because that was an issue last time was several.",
