    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49431.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 17,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, Laniero woodcutters and the Maier d'Amos. The Maier d'Amos are the stewards of the Azekes normally that work with everybody, keep the piece, make sure that they kind of oversee it. And so these are the Maier d'Amos, in this instance is the one, you know, making sure that people are indeed doing the work, that they're passing the certification so they can get paid, all of those things. That's a great model. I love our secures, because our secures are the perfect example of how individuals come together. They work for the common good of protecting their secures, which are then going to provide the private benefit on their own fields. I need to give a shout out. The Asekia Association and the Asekias have been working tirelessly on this issue. They're the ones identifying a lot of the problems that we need to try to fix. They are doing exactly what we'd expect. They're being the voice of their, and they're raising it with me. They're raising it with everybody. A big shout out because the Asekias are leading on raising these issues. They led on, you know, we need to get this emergency extended, which we did. Remember, that's another piece of progress we've made. We extended the FEMA declaration from August 6 to September 6. So we could cover the flooding and we will ask to move it again if we still need. to address the disaster as it's happening, because remember that FEMA declaration, what that does is allow the resources to be deployed to fight the flooding and to pay for the crews out there who are trying to fight the flooding. I have gone, I mean, New Mexico, there is a lot of potholes that might not be fixed on the highways because the Chip and Seal crews are out in Moda County erecting barriers. Like the state of New Mexico has stepped up and they found whatever resources they have and they've put them out there. So we have highway crews erecting barriers in Moda County to try to contain the river and the flooding and they're so happy to be doing it. Partially because it's really beautiful up there still. It is still more is not going to it's not going to give up, you know, it's not going to get up. It is still such a beautiful place, even as you see all the char and those sticks and it breaks your heart, because these are the beautiful places that are the book ends of our History on these lands, but they're going to come back. They're going to come back. We are going to make sure of it. And mother nature is already shooting up. There's ice out baby aspens coming up. I saw oak coming up, grass where didn't burn really hot. The grass is coming up. So we just need to help mother nature come back.",
