  {
    "episode_id": 49431,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49431.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "And yet another little note to our listeners, Asekyas are the historical irrigation ditches that the Spanish brought over centuries ago and that have been collectively managed for hundreds of years. And it's still going strong in many parts of northern New Mexico. And I have to say, I mean, as you talk about it, it's such a beautiful place and it's such a special place. And one of the things that's been happening, I don't know how close you've been to this whole phenomenon, but Basically, vulture capitalists are what they call disaster capitalism. People, corporations are coming in and offering people bottom dollar, essentially, for their land, which they've lived on for many generations. And there's a really good article about this. It's at SearchlightNM.org by Alicia Ines Guzman. Have you have you seen that?",
