  {
    "episode_id": 49431,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49431.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "Yeah. So before we go, and I know you got to go in just a moment, but I just have another question. And that is something that people have been also bringing up to me. And that is that the different agencies like the USDA, the BLM, the EPA, all these that are focused on the land, on places like Northern New Mexico, other places all over the country. tend to be very siloed. Each has its own universe, its own budget, its own programs. They're not necessarily very communicative with one another, but from the perspective of people who are on the land, there might be opportunities from many of those different agencies. And I was wondering if you see a path to more interagency cooperation or staff positions in communities for people, and you've talked about this a little bit, but for people who can really help connect the dots.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
